WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.259
Film Courage: You talked about something in
your book and that’s the magic of making

00:00:03.259 --> 00:00:05.580
people feel special.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.630
And I think we all think we do this ‘the
right way,’ but we probably don’t.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:15.050
So what are some of the ways (aside from complimenting
people, which I think that’s great), but

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.690
how can we show this either verbally or non-verbally?

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:25.350
Carole Kirschner: There are a couple of different
ways, one is actually by listening.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:29.090
Most people…I won’t say most people…many
people don’t listen.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.410
When you are having a conversation with them,
it’s a monologue.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:33.461
“Let me tell you about me.

00:00:33.461 --> 00:00:35.210
Let me tell you about what I did.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.320
Let me tell you about my kid.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.540
Let me tell you about my project.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.780
Let me tell you…”

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:47.120
If you actually, actively listen to somebody,
they feel seen and heard and appreciated.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:54.730
I’m a big fan of gifts, nothing over the
top but I taught my daughter (who went into

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:59.670
be an intern) on the first day you bring snacks,
you know what I mean?

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.989
You bring cookies or muffins or something
else.

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:05.959
Or afterwards you send something to thank
people.

00:01:05.959 --> 00:01:12.139
You thank people genuinely and sincerely for
giving their time because in this business

00:01:12.139 --> 00:01:15.329
that is in short supply.

00:01:15.329 --> 00:01:24.079
So thanking people in a genuine way, not an
a-kissy way, sending a little something is

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:29.029
really nice to do and listening to people.

00:01:29.029 --> 00:01:32.950
Helping people, finding a way that you can
help somebody.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.709
That’s huge.

00:01:34.709 --> 00:01:36.990
That is how you make somebody feel special.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.889
Film Courage: When you do want to thank somebody,
is it a text message, is it via social media?

00:01:41.889 --> 00:01:45.200
Do you Tweet them “Hey, just want to say
thank you, Carole!”

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.299
Or is it an actual email or a card?

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:56.280
Carole Kirschner: If you’re thanking somebody
who is under 25, it can be a text if you happen

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.389
to know their cell number.

00:01:57.389 --> 00:02:04.389
I’m not sure why you know their cell number,
but if you happen to it can be a text.

00:02:04.389 --> 00:02:05.939
It could be a private Tweet.

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:13.400
I personally think it should be an email to
them and it should be within 24 hours and

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.950
it should be a paragraph or less.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:23.620
And if you’ve met with somebody who’s
old school, send a card but don’t have kittens

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.230
on that card.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.750
Don’t have anything too cute on that card.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:34.090
Just your initial or a thank you, very simple.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.620
And I think that works, too.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:41.430
I think you really have to decide who you’re
sending it to.

